WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.090
Here is a perfect example of this issue.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.050
Notice that there are bust
darts in this knit top.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.570
But can you see that there are
wrinkles at the arm hole?

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.570
The bust isn't the issue.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.490
The problem actually is
in the shoulder area.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.330
Here's another example.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.690
Esmeralda, the mannequin,
isn't particularly large busted,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.970
but this blue knit is still showing
wrinkles in the problem area.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.480
Use a piece of cardboard with a 90-degree
angle cut out to determine

00:00:33.546 --> 00:00:37.080
your shoulder slope.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.530
Now, adding a dart won't help this issue.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.240
There are still wrinkles.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.040
But as I pinch out the shoulder,
can you see how I'm removing some

00:00:50.106 --> 00:00:55.320
of the extra fabric in that area
and eliminating the wrinkles?

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.880
As we mentioned in the previous flipbook
pages, you just can't

00:01:00.946 --> 00:01:02.610
add shoulder shaping.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.200
You have to consider the depth of the arm
hole and its relationship

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.200
with the sleeves.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.010
So if your knitting pattern doesn't
already have shaped shoulders,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.250
below are some ideas for adding them
correctly with whatever

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.760
charting method you use.

